' ROULETTE
' From Tim Hartnell's Giant Book of Computer Games (Ballantine Books,
' 1984; Interface Publications, 1983), for the ColecoVision in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' The book's American wheel (0 to 36 and double zero), its croupier who
' speaks only French ("MESSIEURS, FAITES VOS JEUX"), 1,000 chips, one bet a
' spin of the book's sixteen kinds, at the book's odds; a win pays the
' odds and gives the bet back. Run out of chips and you have lost; pass
' 2,000 and you have broken the bank. The second button withdraws from the
' game (the book's Q).
'
' The book's bets and odds (to 1): a number 35 (A), two numbers side by
' side 17 (B), three in a column of the layout 11 (C), six in two columns 5
' (D), four in a square 8 (E), the first, second or third twelve 2 (F, G,
' H), 1-18 and 19-36 1 (I, J), a row of twelve 2 (K), two rows 1/2 (L),
' red, black, even, odd 1 (M, N, O, P).
'
' Controls (either joystick): move the chip over the layout; where it sits
' is the bet: on a number, on the line between two, on a corner, on the
' bottom edge of a column (three) or between two columns (six), on a row's
' arrow (a row) or between two arrows (two rows), on the boxes below.
' Button: bet there; then left and right change the chips by 10, up and
' down by 100; button: spin; second button: back to the layout. Second
' button on the layout: withdraw. Keypad not used.
'
' Liberties taken:
' - The book draws INT(RND * 39) - 1, which can give 37, a number that is
'   not on the wheel (every bet loses); here the ball falls in one of the
'   38 real pockets, each as likely.
' - The book never drew the table or the wheel ("you may well wish to
'   include a spinning wheel as well"): both are drawn here; the ball runs
'   round the wheel in the American pocket order.
' - Bets are placed on the layout instead of typed (A-P), so two numbers,
'   squares and rows must be next to each other on the layout, as at a
'   real table (the book let you type any two numbers for B).
' - The croupier says a little more (RIEN NE VA PLUS). New music (Valse du
'   Croupier) and sounds. The best chip count is kept until power off.
'
' House rules kept: text in columns 2-29 and rows 1-22; sprites: the ball,
' the chip, the marker on the winning number; the frame loop is protected
' from the NMI and every screen is drawn with the NMI off after a wait.

